<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-vectors-parallel-to-plane">
<title>Creating vectors parallel to a plane in <m> \R^3 </m></title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case,
	create two vectors each of which is parallel to the given plane but that are not parallel to each other.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\ell \colon \;\; x - 5 y - 6 z = 1</m></p></statement>

	<answer>

		<p> One such pair of vectors is <m> \uvec{u} = (6,0,1) </m>, <m> \uvec{v} = (1,-1,1) </m>. </p>

		<p>
		<em>Note:</em>
		Any nonzero vector that is a <term><xref ref="definition-vectors-linear-combination" text="custom">linear combination</xref></term> of the two provided answer vectors is a suitable replacement for either of the two provided vectors,
		as long as the final pair of vectors are not scalar multiples of each other.
		</p>

	</answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> \ell \colon \;\; 5 x + 3 y - 4 z = 2 </m></p></statement>

	<answer>

		<p> One such pair of vectors is <m> \uvec{u} = (-1,3,1) </m>, <m> \uvec{v} = (0,4,3) </m>. </p>

		<p>
		<em>Note:</em>
		Any nonzero vector that is a <term><xref ref="definition-vectors-linear-combination" text="custom">linear combination</xref></term> of the two provided answer vectors is a suitable replacement for either of the two provided vectors,
		as long as the final pair of vectors are not scalar multiples of each other.
		</p>

	</answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
